Title:
Patchless AMSI Bypass via Hardware Breakpoints — Implementation Notes

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Describes a “patchless” AMSI bypass that avoids modifying `amsi.dll` by using x64 hardware breakpoints (DR0) on `amsi!AmsiScanBuffer` plus a vectored exception handler (VEH).  
- When the execute breakpoint triggers (`STATUS_SINGLE_STEP`), the VEH rewrites the caller-provided `AMSI_RESULT*` (6th argument at `[RSP+0x30]`) to `AMSI_RESULT_CLEAN`, sets `RAX=S_OK`, and redirects `RIP` to the caller’s return address to skip the function body entirely.  
- This defeats common memory-integrity and signature-based detections that look for inline patches/hotpatches in `AmsiScanBuffer`, since no bytes are written to AMSI code sections.  
- The post covers practical implementation details (MS x64 calling convention, stack layout at function entry, DR6 clearing, Resume Flag usage) and notes limitations like per-thread breakpoint state.  
- It also outlines defender detection avenues: enumerating VEH chains, polling debug registers via `GetThreadContext`, monitoring `NtSetContextThread`, and stack-walk inconsistencies.  
- Useful for red teams/pentesters building in-process loaders, and for blue teams designing detections for breakpoint/VEH-based user-mode tampering.

Technical Focus:
- x86-64 debug registers (DR0–DR7), hardware execute breakpoints, `STATUS_SINGLE_STEP`
- Vectored Exception Handling (VEH) internals and dispatch (`AddVectoredExceptionHandler`)
- MS x64 calling convention and stack argument layout for `AmsiScanBuffer`
- Context manipulation (`CONTEXT_DEBUG_REGISTERS`, `SetThreadContext`, `RIP/RSP/EFLAGS` edits)
- AMSI bypass mechanics (forcing `AMSI_RESULT_CLEAN`, skipping provider invocation)
- Detection strategies for VEH/debug-register abuse

Use Cases:
- Bypass AMSI scanning for in-memory PowerShell/.NET execution without patching `amsi.dll`
- Build “no-inline-hook” evasive loaders that survive basic memory integrity scans
- Extend the same VEH/HWBP pattern to other targets (e.g., ETW-related functions)
- Create defensive telemetry/detections for suspicious VEH registration and DR register usage

Keywords:
AMSI, amsi.dll, AmsiScanBuffer, hardware breakpoints, DR0, DR6, DR7, STATUS_SINGLE_STEP, vectored exception handler, VEH, AddVectoredExceptionHandler, CONTEXT_DEBUG_REGISTERS, SetThreadContext, GetThreadContext, MS x64 calling convention, EFLAGS Resume Flag, RIP/RSP manipulation, ETW, EDR evasion